Building and installing nylex
=============================

nylex needs a C compiler (C89 is enough), python3 to make the
spelling dictionaries (and for "make check"), a Unix-like system with
termios, and a terminal that understands VT100/ANSI escape sequences:
any terminal emulator (xterm, GNOME Terminal, Konsole, iTerm2,
Terminal.app, the Linux and BSD consoles, PuTTY, mintty, tmux,
screen ...).  It is made for 80 columns and 24 lines or more, like
LEX-68.

The usual way:

    make
    make check          a quick test in a pseudo-terminal; needs python3
    make install        as root (sudo, doas); PREFIX=/usr/local

Give the same settings to "make" and to "make install": the place of
the system file is built into the program.

    make PREFIX=/usr
    make PREFIX=/usr install

What is installed:

    $(PREFIX)/bin/nylex, lexvmf, lexspell, lexconv
    $(PREFIX)/share/nylex/nylex.vmf, nylex-en.vmf   (LEXLIB)
    $(PREFIX)/share/nylex/sv.dict, en-GB.dict, en-US.dict   (6.5 MB)
    $(PREFIX)/share/man/man1/nylex.1, lexvmf.1, lexspell.1, lexconv.1
    $(PREFIX)/share/doc/nylex/TUTORIAL.md

Without python3: "make programs" and "make install" as usual; the
spelling menu then says there is no dictionary.  Or make the .dict
files on another machine (they are the same everywhere).

DESTDIR=/some/dir puts all of it under /some/dir, for packages.
"make uninstall" takes it away again.  The first time each user runs
nylex, it copies nylex.vmf (Swedish, when the locale is: sv_SE ...) or
nylex-en.vmf (English, otherwise) to $HOME/nylex.vmf, which then holds
the user's document register.

A new version installs over the old one ("make install" again, with the
same PREFIX); "make uninstall" in the old version's directory first is
needed only if files have gone.  The users' $HOME/nylex.vmf and
nylex.wrk are left as they are: a new system file's menus and help
come with "rm ~/nylex.vmf" (which also empties the document index) or
"nylex -f nylex-en.vmf" for a copy of its own.


Particular systems
------------------

Linux (any distribution; glibc or musl)
    make && sudo make install
    Distribution packages: make PREFIX=/usr; make PREFIX=/usr DESTDIR=... install

FreeBSD, DragonFly BSD
    make && sudo make install           (the system's BSD make is fine)

OpenBSD
    make MANDIR=/usr/local/man
    doas make MANDIR=/usr/local/man install

NetBSD
    make PREFIX=/usr/pkg MANDIR=/usr/pkg/man
    make PREFIX=/usr/pkg MANDIR=/usr/pkg/man install
    (or PREFIX=/usr/local, with MANDIR=/usr/local/man)

macOS
    The compiler and make come with Apple's command line tools:
    xcode-select --install
    make && sudo make install
    With Homebrew on Apple silicon: make PREFIX=/opt/homebrew, and the
    same for make install (no sudo).

illumos (OpenIndiana, OmniOS, SmartOS), Solaris
    make CC=gcc    (or CC=cc with Oracle Developer Studio)
    then make CC=gcc install; GNU make (gmake) works as well.

Haiku
    make PREFIX=/boot/home/config/non-packaged
    make PREFIX=/boot/home/config/non-packaged install

Windows
    Not natively (there is no termios), but in WSL (as Linux), Cygwin
    or MSYS2 (in the MSYS environment) it builds as on Linux.

AIX, HP-UX, other commercial Unix
    make CC=xlc (AIX), make CC=cc CFLAGS=-Ae (HP-UX) or with gcc.

Old System V systems without termios
    make TERMIO=-DUSE_TERMIO       (termio, TCGETA/TCSETAW)

DIAB D-NIX 5.3 (the D-NIX Amiga port's C compiler, /usr/modern/bin/mcc)
    make CC=/usr/modern/bin/mcc CFLAGS=-O TERMIO=-DUSE_TERMIO LEXLIB=/usr/modern/lib/nylex
    The port's image already has nylex in /usr/modern/bin.


Without make
------------

    sh build.sh
    CC=clang CFLAGS=-O2 LEXLIB=/usr/share/nylex sh build.sh

and copy nylex, lexvmf, lexspell, lexconv, nylex.vmf, nylex-en.vmf, *.dict and
doc/*.1 into place.  Or by hand:

    (all of src/*.c but lexvmf.c, lexspell.c and lexconv.c:)
    cc -O2 -DLEXLIB=\"/usr/local/share/nylex\" -o nylex src/main.c src/menu.c \
        src/edit.c src/esc.c src/esc2.c src/cut.c src/calc.c src/rec.c \
        src/input.c src/vdu.c src/ui.c src/sys.c src/listp.c src/print.c \
        src/pdf.c src/work.c src/doc.c src/reg.c src/term.c src/vmf.c
    cc -O2 -o lexvmf src/lexvmf.c src/vmf.c
    cc -O2 -DLEXLIB=\"/usr/local/share/nylex\" -o lexspell src/lexspell.c
    cc -O2 -o lexconv src/lexconv.c
    python3 tools/mkdict.py dict/sv_SE.aff dict/sv_SE.dic sv.dict sv
    python3 tools/mkdict.py dict/en_GB.aff dict/en_GB.dic en-GB.dict en
    python3 tools/mkdict.py dict/en_US.aff dict/en_US.dic en-US.dict en


CMake
-----

    cmake -B build -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=/usr/local
    cmake --build build
    ctest --test-dir build
    sudo cmake --install build

-DUSE_TERMIO=ON for old System V systems; -DLEXLIB=... to move the
system file.


The system file
---------------

nylex.vmf and nylex-en.vmf are made of lexdef.txt and lexdef-en.txt
(the menus, help pages, forms, printers, messages and list processing
formats, in text) by tools/mklexvmf.py; "make vmf" remakes them after a
change (python3).  The printers are in **PNST: 1 the system's lp, 2 the
file utskrift.lst (print.lst in English), 3 a file asked for, 4 the
terminal's printer port, 5 and 6 an ANSI and an Epson printer through
lp, 7 a PDF file asked for (types PDF, PDFSANS, PDFMONO: Times,
Helvetica, Courier).  They can be changed in nylex itself (S F S in the Swedish menus,
Y F P in the English ones: the form for **PNST), as the other
system records can.  LEXPRINT=command sends all printing to a command
instead.  lexvmf(1) lists and changes a system file in place.


Terminal notes
--------------

  * nylex reads the window size when it starts; LINES and COLUMNS
    override it.
  * The Swedish letters: see README.md (nylex -c utf8|latin1|swe7).
  * ESC is a key of its own in LEX-68 (ESC, then the next key).  Alt
    combinations that send ESC first work the same way.
  * Ctrl-S and Ctrl-Q are LEX's keys (the end and the top of the
    document), so nylex turns XON/XOFF flow control off while it runs.
  * Arrow keys, Home, End, Page Up and Page Down work as in VT100/xterm.


Where it has been tried (nylex 0.2)
-----------------------------------

Built and tested ("make check": tests/smoke.py, edtest.py, spelltest.py,
    pdftest.py, convtest.py):
    Linux x86_64: gcc 13, clang, tcc, musl-gcc; GNU make, bmake;
        also with -std=c89 -pedantic -Wall -Wextra -O2 (no warnings)
    Linux (static, musl, built with zig cc) on aarch64, arm, riscv64,
        powerpc, mips and s390x, run under qemu (the last three are
        big-endian)
    DIAB D-NIX 5.3 on the Amiga (DIAB's C compiler), in FS-UAE, also
        side by side with LEX-68 itself on its own system file

Cross-compiled and linked with zig cc, not run:
    macOS x86_64 and arm64; FreeBSD x86_64 and aarch64; NetBSD x86_64;
    OpenBSD x86_64

The rest of the list above follows from what nylex uses (C89, termios,
popen) and has not been tried.  Reports are welcome.
